1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,185
Nineteen aircraft.

2
00:00:02,185 --> 00:00:08,709
Every type Japan's military flies in 2026 — counted, one by one.

3
00:00:09,809 --> 00:00:10,872
Number one.

4
00:00:10,872 --> 00:00:16,570
Two hundred Eagles, licence-built by Mitsubishi since 1981,

5
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:20,723
still the backbone of Japanese air defence.

6
00:00:21,443 --> 00:00:25,468
Ninety F-2s: a bigger wing, heavy anti-ship missiles,

7
00:00:25,468 --> 00:00:28,734
built to break up a landing fleet offshore.

8
00:00:29,454 --> 00:00:36,280
One hundred and forty-seven F-35s planned, with the B models going to sea on Izumo and Kaga.

9
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,239
Only thirty-four Cobras left.

10
00:00:39,239 --> 00:00:43,177
The anti-tank helicopter Japan is quietly retiring.

11
00:00:43,897 --> 00:00:49,179
Twelve Apaches, after costs soared and licence production was halted early.

12
00:00:49,899 --> 00:00:56,726
The Chinook has lifted for Japan since 1986 — artillery, troops, and water over Fukushima.

13
00:00:57,446 --> 00:01:03,112
The Seahawk hunts submarines from destroyers, lowering its dipping sonar into the water.

14
00:01:03,832 --> 00:01:08,510
By August 2025 the whole Osprey fleet had moved to Camp Saga,

15
00:01:08,510 --> 00:01:11,041
closer to the brigade it carries.

16
00:01:11,811 --> 00:01:12,875
Nineteen types.

17
00:01:12,875 --> 00:01:13,585
One fleet.

18
00:01:13,585 --> 00:01:14,861
One island nation.

